New method:
  private boolean waveStay(GameState s, int touched, int target, double qx, double qy, double hx) {
    return p.WAVE_STAY > 0 && touched >= 0 && s.stType[touched] == Const.TOWER && s.stOwner[touched] == 0
        && backEdge(s, target, hx) > backEdge(s, touched, hx)
        && !clearOfKnights(s, qx, qy, p.WAVE_STAY) && firstWave(s, p);
  }
clearOfKnights (lines 365-371) already exists. The ladder block is reached only with threat == 0 (the flight at lines 128-132 catches threat > 0), and LATE_HOLD's target = -1 skips it. RoutePlanner and ApproachPlanner need no change. With 0, there is no change, so the v11 equivalence test holds.

proposals.evidence:
In lost forward races, the walk to the second tower starts about 4.4 turns before the contact, as soon as the first tower reaches TOWER_HP=700. Example, game 917986038: she topped up tower 17 (161 from the back edge) on t13-18, the knights were released at t15, at t19 (knights 953 away) she walked to site 14 (591), and the contact came at 489 with a merge. When forward walks start after the release, the nearest enemy knight is at a median 645 (IQR 349-1004).
Counterfactual of holding at the touched tower when a knight is within R, R = 1000:
- fires in 15 of 29 lost forward races and moves 11 of them within 406 (mean 528 -> 444);
- won forward races: 24 -> 12 beyond 406;
- back races keep their contact half;
- all races beyond 406: 53 -> 30 of 106 (R=800: 29 -> 21 in the lost forward group).
At the contact she would stand a median 123 from her nearest tower, against 247 on the actual walk and 182 on WAVE_BACK's longer walk (394 vs 289). So it removes proposal 1's mid-walk exposure and adds top-ups, whose later value the study notes (917982299). Expected local effect alone: median contact distance about 380, about a third of contacts beyond 406, merges about 18-20 %. It is weaker alone than proposal 1 because the walk often starts before a knight is within R or from a point other than a tower.

